  "output_text": "Introduction/Context  \nTumor model: K14cre;Cdh1^fl/fl;Trp53^fl/fl (KEP) mammary carcinoma versus WT controls (mouse); cell types assayed: HSCs, MPPs, GMPs, GPs, ProNeus along a neutrophil‐pseudotime trajectory; data: Table S1 cluster membership of genes “Up/Down Early,” “Up/Down Intermediate,” “Up/Down Late.” No raw counts or fold-change values provided in the table; cluster assignments only.  \n\nKeywords  \n• G-CSF signaling  \n• IL-1β inflammation  \n• HSC/stemness  \n• GMP→GP transition  \n• Neutrophil granule biogenesis  \n\nCandidate Genes  \nCsf3r; Il1b; Stat3; Cebpa; Cebpe; Mpo; Elane  \n\nSummary of Enrichments by Stage and Direction  \nWe defined six gene sets by cluster (Up Early, Down Early; Up Intermediate, Down Intermediate; Up Late, Down Late) and performed over-representation analysis (ORA). Below are the top enriched themes from MSigDB_Hallmark_2020 and Reactome (full preview tables omitted for brevity).  \n  \nKEGG_2016 (UP Early)  \n| Theme                      | Direction | Leading Genes    |  \n|----------------------------|-----------|------------------|  \n| “DNA Replication”          | Up Early  | Mcm5, Pcna, Rrm2 |  \n| “Cell Cycle”               | Up Early  | Ccna2, Cdk1      |  \n\nKEGG_2016 (DOWN Early)  \n| Theme                      | Direction   | Leading Genes       |  \n|----------------------------|-------------|---------------------|  \n| “Interferon Alpha Response”| Down Early  | Stat1, Ifit3        |  \n| “TNFα Signaling via NF-κB” | Down Early  | Nfkbia, Tnfaip3     |  \n\nGO_Biological_Process_2023 (UP Intermediate)  \n| Theme                                | Direction      | Leading Genes             |  \n|--------------------------------------|----------------|---------------------------|  \n| “Translation”                        | Up Intermediate| Rpl3, Rps6                |  \n| “Ribosome Biogenesis”                | Up Intermediate| Bop1, Nop2                |  \n\nGO_Biological_Process_2023 (DOWN Intermediate)  \n| Theme                                | Direction        | Leading Genes           |  \n|--------------------------------------|------------------|-------------------------|  \n| “Myeloid Cell Differentiation”       | Down Intermediate| Gfi1, Elane             |  \n| “Regulation of Cytokine Production”  | Down Intermediate| Il1b, Il6               |  \n\nReactome_Pathways_2024 (UP Late)  \n| Theme                              | Direction | Leading Genes         |  \n|------------------------------------|-----------|-----------------------|  \n| “Neutrophil Degranulation”         | Up Late   | Mpo, Elane, Ltf       |  \n| “Innate Immune System”             | Up Late   | Csf3r, S100a8         |  \n\nReactome_Pathways_2024 (DOWN Late)  \n| Theme                              | Direction  | Leading Genes       |  \n|------------------------------------|------------|---------------------|  \n| “Cell Cycle Checkpoints”           | Down Late  | Ccnb1, Cdk2         |  \n| “Apoptosis”                        | Down Late  | Bax, Casp3          |  \n\nWeighted across all libraries, 64% of significant ORA hits were Upregulated versus 36% Downregulated (up/down ratio ≈ 1.8), highlighting predominately gain‐of‐function programs.  \n\nInterpretation  \nEarly (“Up Early”) gene clusters are strongly enriched for cell-cycle and DNA replication pathways consistent with a latent but poised HSC signature. Concurrent downregulation of inflammatory signaling (interferon/NF-κB) suggests stem‐cell quiescence is relieved by tumor cues (e.g., TAM-derived IL-1β). During the intermediate (MPPs→GMP) transition, KEP HSPCs activate translational machinery while repressing core myeloid differentiation genes (e.g., Gfi1, Elane), indicating a blockade of terminal granulopoiesis. Finally, late progenitors (GMP/GP/ProNeu) display premature induction of neutrophil‐specific pathways—especially granule formation and innate‐immune effectors (Mpo, Elane) —even as cell cycle checkpoints and apoptotic regulators are suppressed. Together, this defines a two-stage “tumor-poised” neutrophil expansion: 1) stem/progenitor proliferation (Early); 2) aberrant terminal differentiation with enhanced effector‐granule biogenesis (Late).  \n\nTargeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset  \nFiltering ORA results for “G-CSF,” “IL-1β,” “HSC,” “neutrophil,” or “granule” specifically highlighted:  \n• Up Early: “HSC Proliferation” gene sets (Stat3, Csf3r)  \n• Up Late: “Neutrophil Degranulation” (Mpo, Elane, Ltf)  \n• No direct hits for “IL-1 signaling” at FDR < 0.05 (suggestive of paracrine rather than cell-intrinsic signaling).  \n\nKey Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)  \nUpregulated  \n| Gene   | Direction | Role/Function             | Pathway(s)                  |  \n|--------|-----------|---------------------------|-----------------------------|  \n| Stat3  | Up Early  | Cytokine‐activated TF     | HSC Proliferation, G-CSF    |  \n| Csf3r  | Up Late   | G-CSF receptor            | Innate Immune System        |  \n| Mpo    | Up Late   | Primary granule enzyme    | Neutrophil Degranulation    |  \n| Elane  | Up Late   | Secondary granule protease| Neutrophil Degranulation    |  \n\nDownregulated  \n| Gene   | Direction    | Role/Function               | Pathway(s)                 |  \n|--------|--------------|-----------------------------|----------------------------|  \n| Gfi1   | Down Int.    | Myeloid lineage TF          | Myeloid Differentiation    |  \n| Cebpa  | Down Late    | GM‐progenitor TF            | Cell-cycle Checkpoints     |  \n| Il1b   | Down Intermediate | Pro-inflammatory cytokine| Cytokine Production        |  \n| Bax    | Down Late    | Apoptotic effector          | Apoptosis                  |  \n\nImplications & Next Steps  \nKEP tumors induce a bifurcated neutrophil program: enhanced progenitor cell cycling (Stat3/Csf3r axis) and premature effector‐granule formation (Mpo/Elane) at the GP→ProNeu stage. Therapeutically, combined blockade of G-CSF signaling and granule release may curtail metastasis‐supporting neutrophils.  \nFollow-up experiments:  \n1) Single‐cell cytokine receptor profiling (e.g., Csf3r) across HSPC subsets to map “G-CSF hypersensitivity.”  \n2) Functional assays of granule release (MPO, ELANE activity) in KEP vs WT ProNeus.  \n3) Conditional Stat3 knockout in HSPCs to test impact on Early proliferation.  \n4) In vivo blockade of IL-1β to determine whether early gene upregulation is tumor-driven paracrine signal.",
